1
00:00:00,609 --> 00:00:04,972
AI Career Compass helps students who are
starting to explore jobs in

2
00:00:04,984 --> 00:00:05,979
the AI industry.

3
00:00:07,212 --> 00:00:12,110
Job titles alone can be confusing: what
kinds of work exist, where are the

4
00:00:12,110 --> 00:00:14,928
opportunities, and which skills should I
build?

5
00:00:16,113 --> 00:00:20,513
I created this site to make those
questions easier to explore, through

6
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:23,868
job-market visualizations and a personal
skill experiment.

7
00:00:25,546 --> 00:00:26,796
Let me show you how it works.

8
00:00:30,594 --> 00:00:36,475
The dataset is a snapshot of 852 unique US
AI job postings from

9
00:00:36,475 --> 00:00:41,168
107 companies, collected on September
twenty-first, twenty twenty-six.

10
00:00:41,558 --> 00:00:47,171
The sources are public Greenhouse and
Ashby APIs, plus Lever and official

11
00:00:47,171 --> 00:00:48,446
employer career pages.

12
00:00:48,566 --> 00:00:53,821
The collection combines employer lists,
removes duplicate postings, and normalizes

13
00:00:53,821 --> 00:00:55,773
locations and skill names.

14
00:00:56,133 --> 00:01:00,393
It includes different experience levels,
so students can understand

15
00:01:00,406 --> 00:01:01,235
the broader market.

16
00:01:01,861 --> 00:01:04,425
Here is the one-page methodology note.

17
00:01:04,905 --> 00:01:10,747
It distinguishes 795 automatically
collected records from 57 with

18
00:01:10,747 --> 00:01:12,036
curated source review.

19
00:01:12,298 --> 00:01:16,342
Returning to the overview, machine
learning engineering is the largest

20
00:01:16,342 --> 00:01:19,390
direction, followed by infrastructure and
research.

21
00:01:19,865 --> 00:01:24,789
This is a selected sample of accessible
employers, so these distributions describe

22
00:01:24,789 --> 00:01:28,105
our dataset, not the entire US job market.

23
00:01:30,601 --> 00:01:34,861
The first page, Opportunity landscape,
gives students a starting point.

24
00:01:36,095 --> 00:01:40,406
The charts compare job directions,
locations, experience levels,

25
00:01:40,687 --> 00:01:41,809
and recurring skills.

26
00:01:42,809 --> 00:01:44,875
Each uses the same filters at the top.

27
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:51,521
For example, choosing New York narrows the
view to 225 postings and

28
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:52,988
updates every chart.

29
00:01:53,612 --> 00:01:56,150
I can reset the filters to compare another
question.

30
00:01:57,475 --> 00:02:03,546
The experience filter separates Entry,
Senior, Staff, and Manager, with unclear

31
00:02:03,546 --> 00:02:05,574
cases left unspecified.

32
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:11,221
Graduate evidence is a separate filter: an
entry-level label does not automatically

33
00:02:11,382 --> 00:02:14,779
mean a role explicitly welcomes new
graduates.

34
00:02:14,779 --> 00:02:18,950
Below, the skill heatmap compares skill
mentions across directions.

35
00:02:19,974 --> 00:02:24,668
Together, these controls help students
find a direction and location worth

36
00:02:24,681 --> 00:02:27,793
exploring before examining individual
roles.

37
00:02:30,605 --> 00:02:34,278
Skill lab asks what I could learn next,
based on skills I already use.

38
00:02:36,339 --> 00:02:38,354
I select Python and machine learning.

39
00:02:39,602 --> 00:02:45,049
This profile covers 74 of 816 postings
with recorded skills.

40
00:02:45,992 --> 00:02:50,711
To count as covered, at least seventy
percent of a posting's tracked skills must

41
00:02:50,711 --> 00:02:53,390
be selected; four out of five, for
example.

42
00:02:54,915 --> 00:02:57,951
The role bars show where this profile has
coverage.

43
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:01,131
Now I try adding large language models.

44
00:03:02,585 --> 00:03:08,580
The preview rises from 74 to 167 postings:
93 additional

45
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:12,572
postings reach the target, and 247 more
move closer.

46
00:03:14,056 --> 00:03:17,079
I can inspect a practice project before
adding the skill.

47
00:03:18,394 --> 00:03:21,468
When I apply it, the coverage and
recommendations update.

48
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:27,622
This is a way to prioritize learning, not
a prediction of hiring success.

49
00:03:30,618 --> 00:03:33,628
Job postings connects the charts to the
records behind them.

50
00:03:35,629 --> 00:03:40,718
Each row shows the role and company,
experience category, mentioned skills,

51
00:03:40,974 --> 00:03:43,805
location, and official source with its
check date.

52
00:03:45,996 --> 00:03:50,206
Clicking an experience label reveals the
evidence used to classify it.

53
00:03:52,074 --> 00:03:56,959
Selected skills are highlighted, so I can
see what is covered and what is missing in

54
00:03:56,959 --> 00:03:58,234
a specific example.

55
00:04:00,047 --> 00:04:04,958
I can also filter by a skill, such as SQL,
or search a company or title.

56
00:04:06,762 --> 00:04:11,583
Choosing View this slice in charts carries
that selection back to the landscape.

57
00:04:12,932 --> 00:04:17,422
This page is a reference for understanding
the aggregate patterns, rather than

58
00:04:17,422 --> 00:04:18,557
an application portal.

59
00:04:21,046 --> 00:04:25,459
It helps students check the evidence
behind a recommendation instead of relying

60
00:04:25,586 --> 00:04:26,467
on a score alone.

61
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:35,639
AI Career Compass brings three questions
together: where are the opportunities, how

62
00:04:35,741 --> 00:04:39,070
do my current skills relate to them, and
what could I learn next?

63
00:04:40,051 --> 00:04:43,367
The goal is to help students make a more
informed learning and

64
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:44,898
career exploration plan.

65
00:04:45,889 --> 00:04:50,775
The Submission page provides the dataset,
methodology, and reflection, so the

66
00:04:50,787 --> 00:04:51,910
results can be checked.

67
00:04:53,102 --> 00:04:57,719
The site supports exploration; the final
decision stays with the student.
